INT. LIGHTHOUSE-TURNED-WEDDING-VENUE, SEASIDE TERRACE - EVENING

A white tower on a cliff above a violet sea. A terrace strung with lights. A small dance floor. Folding chairs, half of them empty. A cake on a card table.

Six wedding guests drift in the golden hour.

CLEMENTINE "CLEM" OKONKWO-ASHBY. 30s. Maid of honor. Hair coming loose.

ROSS MAKINDE-LINDQVIST. 30s. The best man. A slightly crooked tie.

AUNT ZENOBIA ADEYEMI-HART. 60s. Sequins. Fierce.

YUSUF ABARA-KOWALSKI. 40s. Photographer. A heavy camera.

LENA PETRAKIS-NGUYEN. 20s. A catering waitress with a tray of champagne.

OLD MR. TIBERIUS BRANDT-OSEI. 80s. The groom's grandfather. A cane.

Clem watches the sea. Ross watches Clem.

ROSS
You've been standing there for twenty minutes.

CLEM
I'm thinking.

ROSS
About what?

CLEM
About the speech.

ROSS
You wrote it.

CLEM
That's the problem.

She turns. He smiles. She does not.

AUNT ZENOBIA
Children, the cake!

Clem and Ross speak at the same time.

CLEM
Not yet.

^ROSS
Not yet.

They look at each other. A small, startled grin passes between them.

YUSUF
Smile for me, you two.

They don't. Yusuf lowers the camera.

YUSUF (CONT'D)
Ah. That's the better shot.

CLEM
Don't you dare print that.

YUSUF
I wouldn't dream of it.

Lena glides past with her tray. She offers a flute to Clem. Clem takes one. She offers one to Ross. Ross takes it, and with it, brushes Clem's fingers.

LENA
(murmuring)
Careful. People are watching.

Clem looks across the terrace, past the cake, to the sea. Ross lowers his eyes.

OLD MR. TIBERIUS
Is it time?

AUNT ZENOBIA
It's time, Tiberius.

He taps his cane. A gong sounds from within the tower. Guests turn toward the stairs.

ROSS
We should go in.

CLEM
We should.

Neither moves. Ross sets down his flute. Clem sets down hers. The gong sounds again.

ROSS
Clem.

CLEM
Don't.

ROSS
I haven't said anything.

CLEM
You were going to.

ROSS
I was going to say your speech was good.

CLEM
You haven't heard it.

ROSS
I know you.

She laughs. It is half a sob. He reaches for her hand. She lets him take it for exactly one heartbeat, then pulls free and walks toward the tower.

He stays behind, alone at the rail, watching the violet sea. Lena returns and takes his empty flute.

LENA
She'll come around.

ROSS
She already did.
